I could not finish #628 in this run: the fix is committed and queued to land, but the manager gave no verdict before my time ran out. The issue stays open, and the next run reads the verdict, posts the outcome comment and closes it. If time runs out first, the manager still lands the fix and says so on the issue.

**The fix (commit `a102a8b9`).**
- **Fake tokens:** the chassis redaction tests no longer contain a single literal that looks like a token. The fake JWT is assembled from parts when the test runs, in Go, Java (two tests), Python and Rust.
- **Go page vectors:** the golden strings are unchanged, so they still match the Rust and Java tests. Only their names changed, from `pageTokenVector*` to `pageVector*`: gitleaks flags a long literal when its name contains a word like "token".
- **New test:** `TestNoTokenShapedLiteralInTemplates` checks every template against gitleaks' default `generic-api-key` rule. It reports 12 matches on the old templates and passes with the fix.
- **Scan:** gitleaks over `pkg/templates` now finds no `generic-api-key` matches. It still finds 5 `private-key` matches on the PEM headers in the Python, TypeScript and Rust service-manager templates. That is a different rule from what this issue reports, so I recorded it in PROPOSALS.md as a follow-up.

**Gates (`just gates`, full): overall fail, from two failures already known on main.**
- **current-generation:** failed on the timing test `TestDurableSpoolHoldsRPCWhenPublisherBlocked`, which is #526. Re-run separately, the Go part passed and the Java part passed. The Rust part was still running when time ran out, so it is unverified.
- **memberport:** `HazelcastMemberPortTest.anExpiredTokenDropsTheMemberConnection` timed out. Main has been red on this since #321 (#465).
- **Everything else passed,** including `redact`, which runs the redaction fixtures I changed.

**Trace check:** `looper-trace check` reports 8 findings, all in the `cross-test/server-runtime` Go client, which #311 already tracks. My change touches no handler.

**Patch release:** I did not tag `v2.6.2` on main. Main has taken features since `v2.6.1` (#321, #211), and `v2.6.2` already exists on release/2.6. The patch therefore ships through backport PR #635, whose description names `v2.6.12`.

**Backports:** PRs #629–#635 are open against release/2.0 through release/2.6, each naming the patch tag to cut once it merges.

The final checkpoint is pushed to `looper/tacplus-628`.
